son\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"590b3a2823a633904715820d715e1bfe558b5475f8c792d34a62cfca498a1305","event_uuid":"41e1b5e7-39bf-4033-98fb-6853e67d4374","position":{"commit_sha":"eb898120121df4bebf9880edc610dfa03df3579c","position":"1787806865718875932-41e1b5e7-39bf-4033-98fb-6853e67d4374"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":"claude-code_2-1-246_agent","claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":"2a634c43-3190-4ea8-af29-37e3371d4223","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Implemented as Gerrit change 2256 —\nhttps://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2256 (commit 259a645961fc, branch\n`e668-mutation-sandbox`). Not closing until Verified +1 AND merged.\n\n## What landed\n\n- **`scripts/mutation_sandbox.py`** (144 LOC) — mechanism probe, Seatbelt profile\n  generation, `bwrap` argv construction, fail-closed `wrap()`, and `sandbox_env()`.\n- **`scripts/mutation_gate.py`** — both shard-test-executing subprocesses in\n  `execute_shard` wrapped: the baseline `pytest` and the `mutmut run` mutant test-run.\n  778 LOC, under the 800 cap, which is why the logic lives in its own module.\n- **`tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`** — 14 tests.\n- **`docs/local-dev-env.md`** — operator note on `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED`.\n\n## The mutation check found a real gap in my own tests\n\nAfter wiring, I seeded two defects to check the tests had teeth:\n\n1. Removing `--max-children 1` → the existing concurrency assertion went RED. Good.\n2. **Removing the sandbox wrap from the `mutmut run` call → all 42 tests stayed\n   GREEN.** The enforcement tests proved the *module* denies, but nothing bound it to\n   the call site, so deleting the control was invisible.\n\nThat is the AC this ticket wrote and my first cut did not satisfy. Closed it with\nthree call-site-binding tests (`test_the_mutmut_run_subprocess_is_sandbox_wrapped`\nand siblings). Re-seeding defect 2 now fails with a message naming the incident.\n\n## Enforcement is observed, not asserted\n\nThe live tests execute under the sandbox and check the filesystem afterward rather\nthan inspecting argv — argv assertions would pass against a sandbox that denies\nnothing. Measured on macOS 26.5.2: write outside the allow-list denied and file\nabsent; write inside succeeded; `rm -rf <protected>/*` denied with the target\nsurviving. They skip where no mechanism exists, so Linux `bwrap` enforcement remains\nunverified until the ubuntu CI leg is green — the asymmetry this ticket's ACs state.\n\n## Collateral fix\n\n`tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py`'s `mutmut_run_concurrency` helper required\n`_run`'s first argument to be a literal `ast.Tuple`; the wrapper made it a `Call`, so\nits `--max-children` assertion silently matched nothing. Taught it to look through\nthe wrap — verified still RED when `--max-children` is removed, so the assertion kept\nits strength rather than being loosened.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make lint` 0, `make typecheck` 0, complexity ratchet 0, 56 tests pass across the\nsandbox and existing harness suites.","timestamp":1787801595629876001}],"created_at":1787797410385386001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"2c8973f0-7ac7-4b0f-960b-c0b997c31465","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"fe4e-54a5-3c3a-4901"},{"link_uuid":"1ae5d505-cd8f-494b-9dd9-8534eea7547b","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"6818-615f-555e-4bb9"},{"link_uuid":"ea74f6ab-ed5b-4b30-bca6-e3cb4c3bee36","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"9d51-b267-6884-4197"},{"link_uuid":"7947fdea-e5f3-4b54-bd40-f14ea4b23f0b","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"0a3b-91ab-9532-40c3"},{"link_uuid":"5d4fca18-41d6-488c-a1e0-dc52d4ad1b65","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"f321-cb86-6863-44c7"},{"link_uuid":"4159ad1b-0248-48a1-b47a-5a23fdb1ac61","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"4c38-bb11-0ab7-4354"},{"link_uuid":"41e1b5e7-39bf-4033-98fb-6853e67d4374","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"5fff-aa4f-eeea-438f"}],"description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py` isolates the **source tree** (`git archive` into a temp dir)\nbut not **runtime effects**. On 2026-08-26 a mutation removed a guard from a shell\nscript that performs real deletion, a test exec'd that script with an empty path\nvariable, and the glob expanded to `rm -rf /*` — destroying `/opt/homebrew` and every\nHomebrew-installed app in `/Applications` on a contributor workstation. Only a\n60-second subprocess timeout stopped it; `/Users` was still ahead of it in the target\nlist.\n\nScope is the **OS sandbox only**. The static lint gate that was bundled here was split\nto `6818-615f-555e-4bb9` per this ticket's plan review (finding G5).\n\n## Which subprocess actually matters\n\n`execute_shard()` spawns four subprocesses via `_run()`:\n\n| Line | Subprocess | Executes shard test code? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 579 | baseline `pytest` | **Yes** — unmutated |\n| 583 | `mutmut run --max-children 1` | **Yes — this is the causal path** |\n| 595 | `mutmut results --all true` | No — reporting only |\n| 612 | `mutmut show <mutant>` | No — reporting only |\n\nThe incident occurred under **`mutmut run`**, which executes the *mutated* code.\nSandboxing only the baseline `pytest` would leave the causal path exposed and reproduce\nthe incident exactly. Both test-executing subprocesses are in scope; the two reporting\nsubprocesses are not. `execute_shard()` has exactly two callers, both in\n`mutation_gate.py` (lines 697, 698 — the `base` and `head` runs).\n\n## Why source-tree isolation cannot help\n\ncargo-mutants, mutmut, StrykerJS, PIT and mull all isolate the source tree; **none\nisolates runtime effects.** A copied tree does nothing when mutated code calls\n`rm -rf /` — the syscall still lands. cargo-mutants documents the hazard\n(https://mutants.rs/cautions.html): *\"it might modify existing deletion paths in your\ncode… could potentially delete unintended directories… Run in an isolated environment\n(container, CI, or VM).\"* No mutation tool ships OS-level sandboxing; it is the\ncaller's job. And because the harness string-substitutes the artifact under test, any\nguard **inside** that artifact can be mutated away — only an outside layer is\nload-bearing.\n\n## macOS mechanism: verified, not asserted\n\n`sandbox-exec` is deprecated by Apple, so its enforcement is a load-bearing external\ncapability. **Measured on this host (macOS 26.5.2, `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec`)** with a\nprofile of `(allow default)` + `(deny file-write*)` + an allow-list subpath:\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| write outside the allow-list | **DENIED** — `Operation not permitted`, file not created |\n| write inside the allow-list | succeeded, file created |\n| `rm -rf <protected>/*` (the incident's shape) | **DENIED** — target survived |\n\nDeprecation is a future risk, not a current gap; the fail-closed posture below means a\nfuture breakage surfaces as an abort rather than a silent unsandboxed run.\n\n## Linux mechanism: bubblewrap only\n\n`bwrap` is the committed Linux mechanism. The previously-considered\n`unshare --map-root-user --mount` fallback is **dropped**: `unshare --mount` creates a\nprivate mount namespace but does not deny writes without a read-only remount, and\nunprivileged `CLONE_NEWUSER` is disabled on many hardened hosts — so it could probe as\npresent yet fail to enforce, which is worse than no sandbox because it looks safe.\nWithout `bwrap`, Linux fails closed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Both shard-test-executing subprocesses in `execute_shard()` — the baseline\n      `pytest` (line 579) **and** the `mutmut run` mutant test-run (line 583) — are\n      wrapped in an OS-enforced sandbox denying filesystem writes outside an explicit\n      allow-list (scratch tree, pytest basetemp, the venv, `/dev/null`). Verified by\n      `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`.\n- [ ] The enforcement test trips the deny under **`mutmut run`** specifically, not only\n      under the baseline `pytest`, since `mutmut run` is the path the incident took.\n- [ ] The sandbox demonstrably DENIES: a probe writing outside the allow-list exits\n      non-zero and the file is absent afterward; the same probe inside the allow-list\n      succeeds. Asserting wrapper argv alone does not satisfy this — the test observes\n      enforcement, not construction.\n- [ ] macOS uses Seatbelt `sandbox-exec` with a generated profile, reproducing the\n      three probes measured above. Verified by `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`\n      on a macOS host.\n- [ ] Linux uses `bwrap`, probed at runtime via `shutil.which`. No `unshare` fallback.\n      Verified by a test asserting the probe selects `bwrap` when present and returns\n      \"unavailable\" when absent.\n- [ ] The Linux write-deny is proven **on Linux**, not inferred from the macOS result:\n      the same three-probe enforcement test runs in the `ubuntu-latest` CI leg. This\n      asymmetry is deliberate and stated — the macOS probes below were measured on-host,\n      whereas no Linux host was available at planning time, so `bwrap`'s enforcement is\n      an unverified capability until that CI leg is green.\n- [ ] The sandbox wrapper preserves the existing `mutmut run --max-children 1`\n      concurrency contract asserted at `tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py:948`.\n- [ ] With no sandbox mechanism available the run ABORTS non-zero with a message naming\n      `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED`. Verified by a test stubbing the mechanism\n      absent and asserting that literal appears in stderr.\n- [ ] `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1` waives the sandbox and logs at WARNING; a\n      silent unsandboxed fallback is rejected. Verified by asserting the WARNING record\n      names the same literal.\n- [ ] Enforcement is an OS facility invoked in-process, never a CI-provider feature, and\n      works from a developer laptop with no CI involved (`project.portability`).\n- [ ] `HOME` is set to a non-existent path for both sandboxed subprocesses so adjacent\n      home-directory writes fail loudly. Verified by asserting the child's `HOME`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` — enforcement under both the baseline\n  and `mutmut run`, mechanism probing, fail-closed abort, opt-out WARNING, and `HOME`.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py` — the existing harness suite still passes.\n  It monkeypatches `execute_shard` at lines 489 and 540, and separately asserts\n  `mutmut_run_concurrency(\"execute_shard\")` at line 948 — a concurrency contract on the\n  `mutmut run` invocation (`--max-children 1`) that the sandbox wrapper must preserve,\n  since it wraps exactly that subprocess.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`,\n  `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` — all green.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Every acceptance criterion above is checked by the command it names.\n- The sandbox logic lands in its own module: `mutation_gate.py` is **762 LOC against\n  the 800 hard cap**, so it cannot absorb this inline.\n- Operator note documents `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED` and the risk of setting it.\n\n## Platform scope\n\nPOSIX only. `pyproject.toml:37-38` classifies the project `Operating System :: POSIX`\nand `Operating System :: MacOS :: MacOS X`; the CI matrix\n(`_build-and-test.yml:311`) is `[ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]`. Windows is out of scope\nbecause it is not a supported platform.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from the 2026-08-26 incident. Two other leaks found the same day —\ngate-snapshot accumulation (`a37c-d55c-72c3-439b`) and orphaned CPU load generators\n(`f3d9-ecd8-df72-4fa1`) — are unrelated in cause and tracked separately.\n\nPrior plan-review rounds on this ticket caught three real defects, all corrected above:\nthe `mutmut run` scoping (G6), the unnamed opt-out variable (E2), and the unverified\n`unshare` write-deny claim (T1/T3).","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"scripts/mutation_sandbox.py","reason":"NEW: probe Seatbelt/bwrap, generate the profile, build the wrapper argv; fail-closed"},{"path":"scripts/mutation_gate.py","reason":"wrap BOTH shard-test-executing subprocesses in execute_shard(): baseline pytest (579) and mutmut run (583)"},{"path":"tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py","reason":"enforcement under mutmut run + baseline, mechanism probe, fail-closed abort, opt-out WARNING, HOME"},{"path":"docs/local-dev-env.md","reason":"operator note: REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED and its risk"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["relates_to","0a3b-91ab-9532-40c3"],["relates_to","4c38-bb11-0ab7-4354"],["relates_to","5fff-aa4f-eeea-438f"],["relates_to","6818-615f-555e-4bb9"],["relates_to","9d51-b267-6884-4197"],["relates_to","f321-cb86-6863-44c7"],["relates_to","fe4e-54a5-3c3a-4901"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"f7564e8b-a360-427e-9ccb-c053b185491e","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"in_progress","tags":[],"ticket_id":"e668-b496-e264-4283","ticket_type":"task","title":"Sandbox the mutation-gate test subprocess and gate destructive test-exec","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["0e1b7238-deb9-4076-b4ea-b06c467a0182","5edcf497-c325-430a-a1f1-b5002de4c64e","1ae5d505-cd8f-494b-9dd9-8534eea7547b","c37c172a-8180-485